Layoffs Data Cleaning Using MySQL
Overview

This project focuses on cleaning and preparing a real-world layoffs dataset using MySQL. The objective is to transform raw and inconsistent data into a clean and reliable dataset suitable for analysis and reporting.

The project follows a structured data cleaning workflow, including duplicate removal, data standardization, null value handling, data type conversion, and column cleanup.

Dataset

The dataset contains information about company layoffs, including:

Company Name
Location
Industry
Total Employees Laid Off
Percentage Laid Off
Date
Company Stage
Country
Funds Raised (Millions)
Project Objectives

The primary goals of this project are:

Identify and remove duplicate records.
Standardize inconsistent text values.
Handle missing and blank values.
Convert data types for analytical use.
Prepare the dataset for exploratory analysis and visualization.
Data Cleaning Process
1. Creating Staging Tables

To preserve the original dataset, staging tables were created:

layoff_stage
layoff_stage2

This approach ensures that data cleaning operations do not affect the raw source data.

2. Removing Duplicate Records

Duplicate records were identified using:

ROW_NUMBER()
PARTITION BY
Common Table Expressions (CTEs)

Duplicates were isolated and removed from the staging table to ensure data integrity.

3. Standardizing Data

Several standardization tasks were performed:

Company Names
Removed leading and trailing spaces using TRIM().
Industry Names
Consolidated variations such as:
Crypto
crypto
crypto-related values

into a single standardized value:

Crypto
Country Names
Removed unnecessary punctuation.
Standardized country naming conventions.
4. Date Formatting

The original date field was stored as text.

The following steps were performed:

Converted text values to proper date format using STR_TO_DATE().
Modified the column data type to DATE.

This allows accurate time-series analysis and reporting.

5. Handling Missing Values

The project identifies:

NULL values
Blank values

across multiple columns.

Special attention was given to the industry column.

When a company appeared multiple times and some records contained valid industry information, missing values were populated using self-joins.

Example:

Company A → Industry = Technology
Company A → Industry = NULL

Result:

NULL value replaced with Technology
6. Removing Unnecessary Columns

Temporary columns created during duplicate detection were removed after use.

Example:

row_num

This keeps the final dataset clean and production-ready.

